diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive inspection of every device and interface along the reported fault path, no anomaly exists in this network. PC4 (172.16.10.4/24, VLAN 10 / "ground floor") can reach Laptop0 (172.16.20.10/24, VLAN 20 / "upstairs") in both directions with 0% packet loss and sub-2 ms RTT.

The inter-VLAN routing architecture is a classic router-on-a-stick design: switch1 separates floor-1 hosts (PC4, PC5, Laptop2, Laptop3) into bridge domain `br_vlan10` and Laptop0 into `br_vlan20`; mswitch1 trunks both VLANs upstream to router0 via 802.1Q sub-interfaces (eth0.10 and eth0.20); router0 holds the gateway IPs 172.16.10.1/24 and 172.16.20.1/24, has directly connected routes for both subnets, and forwards between them with kernel IP forwarding — no ACL or iptables rule interferes with this traffic. All physical links and bridge members are UP. The path from PC4 to 172.16.20.1 (the VLAN-20 gateway itself) also succeeds with TTL=64, confirming the trunk and the sub-interface are fully functional.

The helpdesk ticket describes a symptom — inter-VLAN reachability between Location A floors — that is not reproducible in the live lab. The network has been functioning correctly throughout this investigation. The most likely explanation is that the end user experienced a transient issue (e.g., a brief link flap or ARP timeout) that has since self-resolved, or the report was based on a misidentified host. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===